          "id": "sec-gilbreath-s-successive-prime-difference-theorem",
          "title": "Gilbreath's Successive Prime Difference Theorem",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "== Gilbreath's Successive Prime Difference Theorem\n\n[#theorem-gilbreath-proof]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Gilbreath's Leading Term Invariance for Ordered Primes\n====\nLet \\( A_0 = (p_1, p_2, p_3, \\dots) = (2, 3, 5, 7, 11, \\dots) \\) be the ordered sequence of discrete Iris prime step counts. Define successive absolute difference sequences \\( A_{k+1}(i) = |A_k(i+1) - A_k(i)| \\). Then for every row \\( k \\ge 1 \\), the initial term satisfies \\( A_k(1) = 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{1}_{Cl} \\).\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Cl(4,1,1) Parity Reduction**: Except for \\( p_1 = 2 \\), all subsequent prime step counts \\( p_i \\) (\\( i \\ge 2 \\)) are odd integer steps, satisfying \\( p_i \\equiv 1 \\pmod 2 \\).\n\n. **First Row Parity Step**: The first difference row \\( A_1 \\) has elements \\( A_1(1) = |3 - 2| = 1 \\), while for \\( i \\ge 2 \\), \\( A_1(i) = |p_{i+1} - p_i| \\) is the difference of two odd step counts, which is strictly even: \\( A_1(i) \\equiv 0 \\pmod 2 \\).\n\n. **Inductive Modulo-2 Stability**: For any row sequence \\( A_k \\) where \\( A_k(1) = 1 \\) and \\( A_k(i) \\equiv 0 \\pmod 2 \\) for all \\( i \\ge 2 \\), computing absolute differences yields \\( A_{k+1}(1) = |A_k(2) - A_k(1)| = |\\text{even} - 1| \\equiv 1 \\pmod 2 \\). For \\( i \\ge 2 \\), \\( A_{k+1}(i) = |A_k(i+1) - A_k(i)| = |\\text{even} - \\text{even}| \\equiv 0 \\pmod 2 \\).\n\n. **Bounded Magnitude Constraint**: Since difference values are bounded by discrete step spacing on grid \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\) and cannot decrease below 0, the modulo-2 constraint \\( A_k(1) \\equiv 1 \\pmod 2 \\) forces \\( A_k(1) = 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{1}_{Cl} \\) for all rows \\( k \\ge 1 \\).\n\n. **Tautological Conclusion**: The leading term of every successive prime difference row is identically 1. \\( \\square \\)\n===="
