          "contentAsciiDoc": "== Multivector Linear Regression and Gauss-Markov Efficiency\n\n[#theorem-discrete-regression-gauss-markov]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Discrete Gauss-Markov Theorem for Multivector Linear Regression\n====\nGiven a linear discrete observation model \\( Y = X \\beta + \\epsilon \\) on finite grid \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\) with zero-mean, uncorrelated discrete noise \\( \\epsilon \\), the Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) estimator \\( \\hat{\\beta} = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y \\) is the unique Minimum Variance Linear Unbiased Estimator (BLUE) within \\( Cl(4,1,1) \\) under Jaynesian finite-sample constraints.\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Linear Unbiased Class**: Consider any alternative linear estimator \\( \\tilde{\\beta} = C Y \\). The unbiased condition \\( \\mathbb{E}_{\\text{MaxEnt}}[\\tilde{\\beta}] = \\beta \\mathbf{1}_{Cl} \\) requires \\( C X = \\mathbf{I} \\).\n\n. **Variance Decomposition**: Express \\( C = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T + D \\), where \\( D X = \\mathbf{0} \\). The covariance matrix in \\( Cl(4,1,1) \\) evaluates to \\( \\text{Var}_{\\mathcal{I}}(\\tilde{\\beta}) = \\sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1} \\mathbf{1}_{Cl} + \\sigma^2 D D^T \\mathbf{1}_{Cl} \\).\n\n. **Positive Definiteness**: Since \\( D D^T \\) is a non-negative definite multivector operator, \\( \\text{Var}_{\\mathcal{I}}(\\tilde{\\beta}) \\ge \\text{Var}_{\\mathcal{I}}(\\hat{\\beta}) \\), with equality if and only if \\( D = \\mathbf{0} \\).\n\n. **Tautological Conclusion**: The discrete OLS estimator uniquely minimizes aperture variance across all linear unbiased estimators on \\( \\mathcal{G}_N \\). \\( \\square \\)\n===="
